 The insights mentioned above raise a new dilemma. If in the beginning of the run of DMS, the algorithm performs steps that are premature (the tail) and it takes the algorithm many iterations to eliminate their effect, maybe it is better to postpone the splitting operation and allow the algorithm to converge after it has performed damping for a large enough amount of iterations?
 On the other hand, it may be good to allow the algorithm to explore multiple solutions with high potential.
 Thus, we propose a novel family of heuristics, which we call Inter-DMS. In Inter-DMS we interleave the run of DMS, MS-split and DMS-split. We select in advance the order in which we perform these versions of the algorithm. Then, according to the selected order, each of them is executed by the agents for a predefined number of iterations, and then the agents switch to the next version. When the split is performed following DMS, the last message that each function-node sent to each of its neighbors is divided equally between its two copies. Hence, the beliefs of the variable-nodes do not change at the moment of the split. %The first messages that the variable-nodes send after the split are not damped because no earlier message to the copies exists. 
